+| D67 | Chat-mode command usage is delivered by a deterministic trigger over the caller's inbound text, composed via the `/help` runtime path, capped at one block per reply | When a chat-mode caller asks in free text how to do something a command provides ("how do I stop seeing posts from this source" → `/unfollow-source`), the usage block is delivered by **deterministic code**, not by the model electing to deliver it. The trigger lives in `ChatAgent.doHandle` ahead of the LLM call: it embeds the caller's inbound text and probes `doc_embedding` (the M1-664 command-intent corpus) via the SAME `CommandIntentIndex.lookupCommand` entry point the `helpLookup` tool uses, with the caller's visible command set bound as `target_ref = ANY(?)` INSIDE the WHERE (tier-filter-before-return, inherited from D66). A model-elected `helpLookup` call reaches the model context only — never the delivery decision. The trigger's similarity threshold (0.70) is intentionally higher than the tool's (0.60): the tool answers the model's question "which command matches this phrase"; the trigger answers "did the CALLER ask how to do something" — a different question with a higher false-positive cost (an unsolicited usage block on every turn that merely mentions a topic). Both thresholds are pinned as code constants; recalibration is a follow-up. The composed block is the byte-identical output of `HelpCommandHandler.composeUsageBlock` — the same method `/help <cmd>` invokes — plus a fixed bundle-localized header (`CHAT_HELP_DELIVERY_HEADER`). It is appended AFTER sanitize + translate and is one of the two authorized post-sanitize accretions under the M1-663 amendment to security.md §LLM output sanitizer (the other is the D69 topic answer block; topic-over-command precedence caps the pair at one help block per reply): both the emission DECISION (deterministic SQL) and the composed BYTES (fixed bundle keys + closed-list CATALOGUE walk) are deterministic end-to-end. The block is bundle-localized per the scope's `/lang` (the `/help` path's existing behavior), so it does NOT pass through `TranslationPipeline` (D43 two-path rule). At most ONE block per reply: the trigger's SQL is `LIMIT 1`, so at most one match drives at most one composeUsageBlock call. Defense-in-depth: `composeUsageBlock` re-checks tier visibility before composing (a match the SQL tier filter should already have excluded is caught here too — `adminUsageNeverDeliveredToNonAdmin`). The M1-648 r2 INJECTION regression (post-sanitize, model-elected append of privileged command usage) is structurally dead under this design: an attacker-influenced model cannot cause delivery, because the model is never asked and its tool elections do not flow into the trigger. Recorded by M1-665. |
 | D68 | A second `doc_embedding` corpus indexes curated conceptual topics whose answers are bundle-localized reviewed product copy; the staleness guard is split (USER_GUIDE derivation hash OR code-fact pin); topics are tier-flat and namespaced under `topic:<slug>` | The command-intent corpus (D66) lets the bot resolve "which command does X" without inventing syntax, because every command answer composes from the runtime `CATALOGUE`. A second class of question has no such single runtime source — "what is probation", "why can't I post in the group", "what does /forget actually erase", "who can change a source's tags", "unfollow vs delete" — and this row records the corpus that answers them. The corpus is in-code (`HelpTopicCorpus.CORPUS`, a static `List` mirroring the `CATALOGUE` precedent; no CDI needed at class-init), and each topic is a record carrying (a) an intent-shaped MATCH text (title + intent words, mirroring `CommandIntentIndexBuilder.composeIntentText`, NOT the answer body — matching a short user question against a long answer embedding is asymmetric and under-recalls the tail phrasings this feature exists to serve; HyDE/query-rewrite are out-of-scope per D19, so the match surface must itself be question-shaped) and (b) a served ANSWER as a bundle key (`topic.<slug>.answer`, D43 en/cs pair; D30 plain text; bundle-localized answers deliberately bypass `TranslationPipeline` per the D43 two-path rule, same as the D67 usage block). The served answer is reviewed product copy, never a raw USER_GUIDE.md slice — so the runtime depends on no markdown heading structure. **Match-not-assert (D66) carries to topics:** `CommandIntentIndex.lookupTopic` returns a POINTER only (the topic slug); the answer is composed at delivery time (M1-666) from the in-memory corpus, so a stale or attacker-edited topic row can degrade a match but can never produce wrong served text. **Staleness guard is split**, because a single guard shape cannot cover both topic kinds: (a) conceptual (mental-model, rationale) topics keep a USER_GUIDE.md derivation hash — each records the section anchor (a stable HTML-comment id, NOT heading text, so a heading reword does not red the build for a non-reason) plus a SHA-256 of the bytes between the markers, and `HelpTopicCorpusTest.topicDerivationHashesMatchCurrentUserGuide` reds the build when the guide drifts under a topic; (b) code-fact topics pin the FACT to the runtime source, not the guide — `HelpTopicCorpusTest.forgetErasureTopicMatchesPurgeService` asserts the `/forget` topic enumerates exactly the categories `ForgetPurgeService.PurgeResult`'s record components derive (chat_memory, chat_session, summary_anchor personal-only, saved_post global-per-D13), and `HelpTopicCorpusTest.probationTopicDurationMatchesApplicationConfig` asserts the probation topic's stated duration matches `infochat.probation.duration` from the main `application.properties`. The guide hash alone detects guide CHANGE, not guide/code MISMATCH, so it would not have caught either USER_GUIDE defect M1-649 also fixes; the code-fact pin catches the drift the guide hash CANNOT — code changing while the guide (and the topic) stay stale-green. **Tier-flat by construction:** every topic is user-tier; `HelpTopicCorpusTest.noTopicReferencesAdminSurface` asserts no topic (match text OR answer value, EN or CS) names a `HelpCommandHandler.HelpTier.BOT_ADMIN` command. The pin is against the bot-admin surface, NOT against `LlmOutputSanitizer.CLOSED_LIST` membership — the group-admin commands topics must name (`/add-source`, `/lang`, `/follow-tag`, …) are themselves `CLOSED_LIST` entries and are expected in topic text. This is necessary but NOT sufficient for delivery safety: the user-reachable commands topics must name are in the sanitizer `CLOSED_LIST`, which is exactly why M1-666 delivers deterministically post-sanitize rather than through the model. **doc_id namespace disjointness:** every topic `doc_id` is namespaced (`topic:<slug>`); V60's PRIMARY K